+## C117: The deployed learned policies were trained with a 5-cycle decision hold and are evaluated at 1-cycle hold
+- **Statement**: Every learned arm in the IROS ladder study (rl_kh, rl_h, rl_k, rl_kh_masked, both policy seeds) loads a checkpoint from `sac_route_independent_2026-09-10`, whose archived trainer chooses one (K,H) pair and then applies it for five consecutive control cycles (`env.step(policy)` followed by `for _ in range(4): env.step(holder['pair'])`). The evaluation roller re-queries the actor every cycle and asserts `len(decisions) == len(rows)` (kh_ladder_roll.py:130-133), which enforces the opposite protocol. A hold-1 trainer exists in the working tree (`--decision-hold`, stage `sac_route_independent_realtime_2026-09-16`) but that stage directory does not exist on disk: no hold-1 checkpoint has ever been trained. Because the actor forward pass is inside the end-to-end latency meter (environment.py:47-51, `meter.mark('policy')` after the actor returns) and `elapsed = last - start`, the learned arms pay actor inference cost on every cycle here versus every fifth cycle in training, under a delay mode where that cost becomes physical actuation latency.
+- **Status**: supported
+- **Provenance**: ai-suggested
+- **Falsification criteria**: The archived source copy shipped inside the checkpoint directory does not contain the 5-cycle inner loop, or `sac_route_independent_realtime_2026-09-16` is found to contain completed checkpoints, or the latency meter is shown to exclude the policy segment. The MAGNITUDE of the effect is NOT established: no hold-1-trained policy exists to compare against, and the per-cycle actor inference cost has not been extracted from `segment_latency_s` because the sweep guard forbids trace reads while rolls are alive. The direction is argued, not measured, and both identified mechanisms (distribution shift from deciding 5x more often; 5x more inference cost inside the measured latency) run AGAINST the learned arms -- so this cannot have manufactured an advantage for our method, but it does mean the learned arms' compute figures are an upper bound on what the method intrinsically costs.
+- **Proof**: [`research_artifacts/kh_scene_strata_2026-09-23/deviation_train_deploy_2026-09-23.json`, `research_artifacts/icode_sac_compute_2026-09-08/sac_route_independent_2026-09-10/seed13101001/joint/sources/experiments/icra_sac_route_independent/train.py`, `experiments/icra_scene_design/kh_ladder_roll.py`, `src/mobile_robot_mppi/physical_tradeoff_v2/environment.py`]
+- **Dependencies**: [C115]
+- **Tags**: methodology, train-deploy-mismatch, decision-hold, deviation-disclosed, bias-runs-against-us, magnitude-unmeasured
